dict(img=None,
stem="An investigator studies left ventricular function in an anesthetized dog. Stroke volume is plotted against left ventricular end-diastolic pressure across a range of infused volumes, generating a Frank–Starling curve. A dobutamine infusion is then started together with an arterial vasodilator that lowers systemic vascular resistance by 30%, and the measurements are repeated.{FIG}Compared with baseline, the new curve is most likely shifted in which of the following directions?",
choices=["Downward and to the right","Upward and leftward from baseline","Along the same curve, to the left","Along the same curve, to the right"], key=1,
exp="Lecture (Starling curves): <b>venous return sets where on a curve</b> the heart sits; <b>afterload and inotropy set which curve</b>. <b>↑ Inotropy (dobutamine) + ↓ afterload (vasodilator)</b> → a higher stroke volume at any filling pressure → the curve shifts <b>up and to the left</b>. ↑ Afterload + ↓ inotropy (failing heart) → <b>down and to the right</b>. This is the logic of inotropes and afterload reduction in ADHF.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> More contractility and less afterload shift the Frank–Starling curve up and to the left.",
wrong={0:"Would be correct for ↑ afterload and/or ↓ contractility (failing heart, vasoconstriction).",
2:"Would be correct for ↓ preload alone (diuresis), moving down the same curve.",
3:"Would be correct for ↑ preload alone (volume infusion), moving up the same curve."},
eli5="Making the heart squeeze harder and easing the pressure it pumps against lets it push out more blood at every filling level, so the whole curve moves up."),

